# microdns

*microdns, because small services can also create large outages.*

A minimalist authoritative DNS server. It serves fixed records from a small
zone file and everything else from memory: you add records while it runs by
calling an HTTP(S) API, authenticated with a shared-secret HMAC.

```sh
cabal run -- microdns plain \
  --dnsPort 5354 --dnsApex example.com. \
  --webPort 3333 --webHmacSecretFile secret-web-hmac-file.txt \
  --zoneFile fixtures/example.mzone
dig +short @127.0.0.1 -p 5354 example.com
```

The code is on GitHub at
[`lucasdicioccio/microdns`](https://github.com/lucasdicioccio/microdns). It
is a Haskell cabal project: the `microdns` library and the `microdns`
executable.

## What problem does it solve?

The zeroth use-case is dynamic DNS of one's own, to track and automate some
configurations. Another is to answer
[ACME DNS-01 challenges](https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc8555#section-8.4)
from [Let's Encrypt](https://letsencrypt.org). More generally, microdns fits
the cases where you want to expose something on top of DNS.

microdns is built on the
[prodapi](https://github.com/lucasdicioccio/prodapi) package, which gives it
some metrics and a somewhat uniform way of building services.

Dynamic records live in memory only: they are lost when the server restarts.
DNSSEC is unlikely (but why not), and PunyCode will come if some user asks
politely.

## How it fits together

![A DNS query goes through MicroDNS.Server, the DAI interface and MicroDNS.Handler, which looks records up in the zone file and in the dynamic registrations](/gen/images/design.dot.png)

The implementation resembles a modern HTTP server: a server API
(`MicroDNS.DAI`), much like
[WAI](https://hackage.haskell.org/package/wai), separates servers from
handlers. A single-threaded reference implementation (`MicroDNS.Server`)
serves DNS over UDP. Helpers (`MicroDNS.Handler`) implement applications
based on lookup functions, so that you merely have to provide an
`IO [DNS.ResourceRecord]`. Finally, `MicroDNS.MicroZone` defines a zone-like
parser and `MicroDNS.DynamicRegistration` provides the HTTP(S) configuration
interface.

